public with sharing class FollowUpTaskService {

    /**
     * Creates a follow-up Task on each of the supplied opportunities.
     *
     * A single bulk insert with allOrNone = false is used so that a task
     * rejected by a compliance validation rule does not prevent the other
     * tasks from being created. The Ids of the opportunities whose task could
     * not be created are returned; an empty set means every task was created.
     *
     * @param  opportunityIds Ids of the opportunities to create follow-ups on
     * @return Ids of opportunities whose follow-up task was not created
     */
    public static Set<Id> createFollowUps(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Set<Id> failedOpportunityIds = new Set<Id>();
        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        // One query for everything we need (name + owner), no query per row.
        Map<Id, Opportunity> opportunities = new Map<Id, Opportunity>(
            [SELECT Id, Name, OwnerId FROM Opportunity WHERE Id IN :opportunityIds]
        );

        // Build all tasks first, then insert them in a single DML statement.
        List<Task> tasks = new List<Task>();
        for (Opportunity opp : opportunities.values()) {
            tasks.add(new Task(
                WhatId = opp.Id,
                OwnerId = opp.OwnerId,
                Subject = 'Follow up: ' + opp.Name,
                ActivityDate = Date.today().addDays(7),
                Status = 'Not Started',
                Priority = 'Normal'
            ));
        }

        // allOrNone = false -> partial success: one rejected task (e.g. a
        // compliance validation rule) does not block the rest.
        List<Database.SaveResult> results = Database.insert(tasks, false);
        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            if (!results[i].isSuccess()) {
                failedOpportunityIds.add(tasks[i].WhatId);
                for (Database.Error err : results[i].getErrors()) {
                    System.debug(LoggingLevel.WARN,
                        'Follow-up task rejected for opportunity ' + tasks[i].WhatId +
                        ': ' + err.getMessage());
                }
            }
        }

        return failedOpportunityIds;
    }
}
```

## What changed

- **`Database.SavepointResult` → `Database.SaveResult`.** `Database.insert(List<SObject>, Boolean)` returns `List<Database.SaveResult>`. `SavepointResult` is not a valid Apex type — `SavepointResult`/`Database.SavepointResult` only appears in the `Database.Savepoint` and `Database.Rollback` context, not as a DML result. `SaveResult` exposes `isSuccess()` and `getErrors()`, which is what the loop uses.

Everything else is unchanged: one bulk insert with `allOrNone = false` keeps us at a single DML statement (safe across your 150–250 range, well under the 150‑DML limit), partial success lets validation‑rule‑rejected tasks fail individually without blocking the rest, and the failed results map back to their opportunity Ids.

