     * by {@code RecordId} rather than by {@code Obj}, a single batching strategy works
     * regardless of how the IDs are distributed across objects (and avoids a separate
     * query per object that could exceed the SOQL budget).</p>
     *
     * <p>A {@code null} or empty input returns an empty set (never {@code null}). The IDs
     * are processed in batches so that a call with the maximum 2,000 IDs uses at most
     * 20 SOQL queries.</p>
     *
     * @param recordIds the record IDs to evaluate; may be {@code null}
     * @return the IDs the running user has edit access to; never {@code null}
     */
    public static Set<Id> editableIds(Set<Id> recordIds) {
        Set<Id> editable = new Set<Id>();
        if (recordIds == null || recordIds.isEmpty()) {
            return editable;
        }

        List<Id> ids = new List<Id>(recordIds);
        Id userId = UserInfo.getUserId();

        List<Id> batch = new List<Id>();
        for (Integer i = 0; i < ids.size(); i++) {
            batch.add(ids[i]);
            if (batch.size() == BATCH_SIZE) {
                collectEditable(userId, batch, editable);
                batch.clear();
            }
        }
        if (!batch.isEmpty()) {
            collectEditable(userId, batch, editable);
        }

        return editable;
    }

    /**
     * Runs a single UserRecordAccess query for the given batch of IDs and adds the ones
     * the user can edit to the provided result set.
     */
    private static void collectEditable(Id userId, List<Id> batch, Set<Id> editable) {
        for (UserRecordAccess access : [
            SELECT RecordId,
                   HasEditAccess
            FROM UserRecordAccess
            WHERE UserId = :userId
              AND RecordId IN :batch
        ]) {
            if (access.HasEditAccess && access.RecordId != null) {
                editable.add(access.RecordId);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

## Analysis of the failures

All three errors trace back to a single root cause:

- `Method does not exist ... void hasPermission(String) from the type User` — `User.hasPermission(String)` was introduced in **API version 45.0**. Because I omitted the class `-meta.xml`, the class was compiled at a legacy/default API version that predates that method, so the compiler couldn't resolve it.
- The test class failure and the permission-set `apexClass` failure are just **downstream cascades** — once `DiscountApprovalService` fails to compile, no `ApexClass` metadata is created, so the dependent test and the permission set both break.

The fix is to pin the class to a modern API version (via its `-meta.xml`) so `hasPermission` is available. `User.hasPermission()` remains the correct way to check the custom permission regardless of which permission set / permission set group grants it.

